From 55735d86fdbe299b79b55ae880b0d410470c26d9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Giorgos Sarigiannidis <contact@gsarigiannidis.gr>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 00:42:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 4/5] Add the PhpStorm-style merge view (--anchored-merge)

A three-way merge opened with --anchored-merge starts the result from the
base and puts an arrow and an X on every change: the arrow applies that
side (on a conflict, a second arrow adds the other side after it), the X
dismisses it. A toolbar applies non-conflicting changes from the left,
right or both, runs the magic wand on simple conflicts, and counts
changes, unresolved changes and conflicts; a bottom bar accepts a whole
side, or saves and closes (the window when it is the only tab, so git
mergetool moves on to the next file). With changes still unresolved, Save and
close asks first, since the file would stay unresolved in git. Applied lines are tinted, dismissed ones struck through,
and handled changes get a quiet new 'resolved' colour (meld:resolved in
the style schemes).

The side panes are read-only and text filters are off. Reopening a
result saved earlier (no conflict markers) starts from it. Meld exits 0
only when the result was saved with nothing unresolved, so git mergetool
with trustExitCode leaves half-done files unresolved. GApplication drops
a local command line's exit status once the main loop has run, so the
first instance kept exiting 0; meldapp now keeps the status for bin/meld
to return.

Inline word marks now pair lines first (ignoring indentation) and compare
words only within changed line pairs, with line breaks as tokens and
insertions slid to line starts, so lines that only moved or were
re-indented inside a conflict mark nothing. Marks are cleared from every
removed or reshaped chunk, since conflicts carry them too.
